WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.000 --> 00:00:08.810
This is Shiplog: a personal dev board that keeps itself in sync with GitHub. It follows one loop. Plan, code, log, and share.

2
00:00:09.510 --> 00:00:20.930
Sign in with GitHub, then choose which repos Shiplog can read through a GitHub App. Every repo becomes a project, and your real contribution year sits on top. Click any day to see what you worked on.

3
00:00:21.630 --> 00:00:29.550
Plan. Each project gets its own kanban board. Open GitHub issues sync in as cards, right next to your private to-dos.

4
00:00:30.250 --> 00:00:42.420
Code. Signed GitHub webhooks move the cards for you. Open a pull request that says fixes number twelve, and card twelve moves to in progress. Mark it ready, it's in review. Merge it, and it's done.

5
00:00:43.120 --> 00:00:51.400
Prefer 3D? The galaxy view turns every column into a planet, with its cards orbiting as little ships you can fly between them.

6
00:00:52.100 --> 00:01:05.440
Log. Every evening, Gemini turns the day's commits, issues and pull requests into a short journal entry you can edit. Each week becomes a summary with CV-ready bullet points. Private repos never leave the app.

7
00:01:06.140 --> 00:01:13.820
Share. Switch on a public page, and anyone can upvote your roadmap and read a changelog that your merged pull requests write for you.

8
00:01:14.520 --> 00:01:23.570
Plan, code, log, share. That's Shiplog, built with Next.js, TypeScript, Prisma and Postgres, and deployed on Vercel.
